# The installer's temporary unlock of a TARGET's encrypted data volume.
#
# `sentinel install --encrypt` opens the new LUKS volume on the target disk as
# /dev/mapper/sentinel-install-data, makes the LABEL=data ext4 inside it, and
# closes it again (`sentinel install` and the wizard's configuration seed). The
# box it runs on mounts its own state by that same label. Left to the disk rules,
# the open target briefly owned /dev/disk/by-label/data; when it closed, the
# alias went away under the running box's /var/lib/sentinel, systemd stopped the
# mount (its fsck unit is bound to that device) and with it every service that
# needs the state partition.
#
# Sorted after 10-dm.rules (which names the device) and before 13-dm-disk.rules
# (which would add the by-label/by-uuid links): no disk links and no systemd
# device unit for this one mapper name. Its /dev/mapper node is unaffected.
SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="dm-*", ENV{DM_NAME}=="sentinel-install-data", ENV{DM_UDEV_DISABLE_DISK_RULES_FLAG}="1", ENV{DM_UDEV_DISABLE_OTHER_RULES_FLAG}="1"
